# Drilling Bonus System – Cline Rules (v2 – Excel first)

You are building a modular web app that calculates drilling employee bonuses.

## Current Data Sources
- Workera API → Attendance
- Excel files (one per Rig) → Date, Hole, Shift (Day/Night), Drilling Level, Meters, Hours
- Crew Assignment Excel → Which employees worked on which Rig/Machine

## Future
CorePlan will later replace the Excel files.  
Design must stay modular so switching providers requires minimal changes.

## Tech Stack (Mandatory)
- Next.js 15 (App Router) + TypeScript
- Tailwind + shadcn/ui
- Prisma + PostgreSQL
- Zod + date-fns
- Excel parsing: exceljs or xlsx
- Auth: NextAuth.js or Clerk

## Architecture Rules
- Treat data sources as interchangeable providers
- All calculation logic works only on normalized internal models
- Calculation engine must be pure functions (no DB or API calls)
- Excel parsing stays isolated in `lib/excel/`
- Future CorePlan code will live in `lib/coreplan/`
- Never hard-code percentages or formulas

## Folder Structure
src/
├── app/(dashboard)/
│   ├── sites/
│   ├── employees/
│   ├── uploads/
│   ├── rules/
│   ├── calculations/
│   └── sync/
├── lib/
│   ├── workera/
│   ├── excel/
│   ├── coreplan/          ← future
│   ├── providers/
│   ├── matching/
│   ├── bonus-engine/
│   └── db.ts
├── prisma/
└── types/

## Coding Rules
1. Never hard-code bonus rules
2. Strict TypeScript – avoid `any`
3. Always store data source ("workera" | "excel" | "coreplan")
4. Store full input snapshot with every calculation (audit)
5. Prefer configuration over new code
6. Keep business logic out of React components

## Current Priority (Phase 1 + 2)
1. Prisma schema
2. Workera client (employees + attendance)
3. Excel upload + parsing (Rig files + Crew Assignment)
4. Normalized models
5. Basic matching

If a request conflicts with this document → stop and ask.